<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Encyclopédie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Encyclopédie was a French encyclopaedia edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> and, until 1759, Jean le Rond d'Alembert, published in 17 volumes of text and 11 of plates between 1751 and 1772. With contributions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and many others, it aimed to gather all knowledge and spread critical, rational thought. It was repeatedly banned by the authorities and became a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/encyclopedie.jpg" alt="Encyclopédie" width="320" height="523" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Long List of Contributors to the Encyclopédie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Encyclopedie_de_D%27Alembert_et_Diderot_-_Premiere_Page_-_ENC_1-NA5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Encyclopédie, ou dictionnaire raisonné des sciences, des arts et des métiers began as a French translation of Ephraim Chambers's Cyclopaedia, first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1728. Around 1745 the Englishman John Mills and the German Gottfried Sellius undertook a translation that attracted the Parisian bookseller and printer André Le Breton. A prospectus issued early in 1745 gave the work, for the first time, the title Encyclopédie and promised five volumes by the end of 1748. The project grew far beyond that plan under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> and, until 1759, Jean le Rond d'Alembert, and more than 140 people contributed at least one article.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>D'Alembert's Preliminary Discourse set out a taxonomy of knowledge, adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-bacon">Francis Bacon</a>, in three branches: memory or history, reason or philosophy, and imagination or poetry. It made human reason, not religious or other authority, the measure of knowledge. The work was the first general encyclopedia to describe the mechanical arts in detail and the first to carry articles by named contributors. Much was compiled from other works, above all the Cyclopaedia.</p><p>Contributors included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, and the most prolific was Louis de Jaucourt, who wrote 17,266 articles, about a quarter of the whole. They were not a unified party but a varied group of men of letters, physicians, scientists and craftsmen. Articles such as Political Authority traced authority to ordinary people rather than to divinity. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> the work was ambivalent: some authors defended it, while Jaucourt attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first edition had a print run of 4,225 copies, well above the average for French books. After the second volume, a royal edict of 1752 briefly suspended it, accusing it of destroying royal authority. In 1759 the government suspended its privilege on the initiative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, but work continued in secret, helped by supporters including the minister Malesherbes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madame-de-pompadour">Madame de Pompadour</a>.</p><p>By 1789 some 25,000 sets had been sold across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in its various editions, though the high price of the first edition, 980 livres, kept it from much of the middle class. Extracts, including the Preliminary Discourse, appeared in several languages. Its prestige helped establish encyclopedia as the general name for such works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Encyclopédie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of Montesquieu, Voltaire, Rousseau and Diderot's <b>Encyclopédie</b> was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Denis Diderot wrote that the purpose of the <b>Encyclopédie</b> was “to change the way people think”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
